get_selected_method() had its test the wrong way round: a client's
LOCATION_METHOD_USER_SELECTED (0, what most apps and liblocation's example
ask for) gave no method at all, a specific method every method. After
the "enable positioning" dialog (the only place that uses it on a start)
the user's yes therefore ended in "User didn't enable requested methods",
with GPS just switched on. Now 0 stands for every method the user allows
(cell positioning only with the network allowed, so that a dialog
answered with nothing still fails), a specific one for itself, as the code
below the test already treats them. The same code is in Leste's
liblocation.

--- a/src/location-gpsd-control.c
+++ b/src/location-gpsd-control.c
@@ -183,11 +183,13 @@
 	int acwp, gnss, agnss, result;
 	LocationGPSDControlPrivate *p = location_gpsd_control_get_instance_private(control);
 
-	if (method) {
+	/* LOCATION_METHOD_USER_SELECTED (0): every method the user allows
+	 * (cell positioning, CWP, being a network one) */
+	if (!method) {
 		acwp = LOCATION_METHOD_ACWP;
 		gnss = LOCATION_METHOD_GNSS;
 		agnss = LOCATION_METHOD_AGNSS;
-		result = LOCATION_METHOD_CWP;
+		result = p->net_disabled ? 0 : LOCATION_METHOD_CWP;
 		goto out;
 	}
 
